00:43:00,160 --> 00:43:05,920
근데 몰입도가 정말 엄청나잖아요.
그래서 소비자 쪽에서는—
But it's very, very engaging, right? And so, on the consumer side—

749
00:43:05,920 --> 00:43:11,040
저희랑 같이 일하는 회사들 중에
피카나 헤드라 같은 곳들이
Some of the companies we work with, like Pika Labs and Hedra, they're focusing on real-time video generation—that's the bet, right?

750
00:43:11,040 --> 00:43:16,000
실시간 영상 생성에 집중하고
있어요, 그게 베팅인 거죠.

751
00:43:16,000 --> 00:43:17,680
풀어야 할 완전히 새로운
문제들이 있는 거죠.
There's a whole new set of problems to solve.

752
00:43:17,680 --> 00:43:18,560
맞아요, 맞아요.
Right, right.

753
00:43:18,560 --> 00:43:22,602
그러니까 그냥 컴퓨트가 훨씬
더 많이 드는 거예요—영상
And so it's just a lot more compute—like video generation is very, very demanding on the compute side.

754
00:43:22,602 --> 00:43:26,240
생성은 컴퓨트 쪽으로 정말
정말 부담이 크거든요.

755
00:43:26,240 --> 00:43:31,119
그래서 그게 칩 수요도, 추론 최적화
수요도 더 키우고 있을 수 있어요.
So that might be fueling even more chips, even more inference optimization.

756
00:43:31,119 --> 00:43:31,839
정말 흥미롭네요.
Super interesting.

757
00:43:31,839 --> 00:43:33,656
그러니까, 당연히 지금 많은 연구를
I mean, obviously, you're, you know, conducting a lot of research and making these bets ahead of—

758
00:43:33,656 --> 00:43:35,760
하시면서 앞서서 베팅을 하고 계신 건데—

759
00:43:35,760 --> 00:43:38,319
이 분야가 어디로 향할지에 대한
큰 미해결 질문들이 많잖아요.
There's all these big outstanding questions of where the space is headed.

760
00:43:38,319 --> 00:43:41,839
궁금한 게, 만약에 3년
뒤 미래로 순간이동을 하셔서
I'm curious, if, like, if you could, you know, fast-forward to the future 3 years from now,

761
00:43:41,839 --> 00:43:44,656
AI 인프라 세계에서 질문
하나의 답을 얻을 수 있다면,
and get the answer to, like, one question in the AI infrastructure world that would really help you shape your direction today.

762
00:43:44,656 --> 00:43:47,200
지금 방향을 잡는 데 정말
도움이 될 만한 걸로요.

763
00:43:47,200 --> 00:43:49,538
지금 가장 궁금하거나,
앞으로 연구 방향에
Like, what's that question that's outstanding that you're most curious to know, or would most impact the direction of your work?

764
00:43:49,538 --> 00:43:52,079
가장 큰 영향을 줄 그
미해결 질문이 뭔가요?

765
00:43:52,079 --> 00:43:55,267
제가 앞으로 몇 년간
풀려는 질문은, AI를
The question I'm trying to answer for the next couple years is how do we get AI to expert level?

766
00:43:55,267 --> 00:43:58,720
어떻게 전문가 수준까지
끌어올릴까 하는 거예요.

767
00:43:58,720 --> 00:44:03,881
지금은 모델들이 프론트엔드
프로그래밍 같은 몇몇
Right now, I think the models are at sort of the median human level on some of the tasks, like front-end programming.

768
00:44:03,881 --> 00:44:09,440
작업에서 대략 중간 정도의
사람 수준인 것 같아요.

769
00:44:09,440 --> 00:44:12,822
사실 꽤 잘하긴
해요—프론트엔드 프로그래밍은
They've gotten quite good, actually — the models are much better than me at front-end programming, for sure.

770
00:44:12,822 --> 00:44:15,359
저보다 모델이 확실히 더 나아요.

771
00:44:15,359 --> 00:44:22,800
아니면 데이터 분석처럼, 인터넷에
데이터가 많은 종류의 작업도 그렇고요.
Or, you know, analyzing data — that kind of task where there's lots of data on the internet.

772
00:44:22,800 --> 00:44:26,880
인터넷에 데이터가 많기만 하면 이
모델들은 그냥 압도적으로 잘해요.
Whenever there's lots of data on the internet, these models are going to just crush it.

773
00:44:26,880 --> 00:44:30,303
그래서 그런 작업들에서는
대략 중간, 어쩌면
So they've gotten to sort of the median, or maybe slightly above average level, on some of these tasks.

774
00:44:30,303 --> 00:44:33,440
평균보다 살짝 위 정도까지는 온 거예요.

775
00:44:33,440 --> 00:44:35,688
그런데 경제적으로 가치 있는 작업들은
But I think the economically valuable tasks are still there, right?

776
00:44:35,688 --> 00:44:38,160
아직 그대로 남아 있다고 봐요, 그렇죠?

777
00:44:38,160 --> 00:44:43,640
우리는 인간 전문가한테 많은
돈을 지불하잖아요—비행기
We pay lots of money for human experts in — I don't know — designing airplanes, or designing hardware, or, you know, doctors and lawyers and so on, right?

778
00:44:43,640 --> 00:44:49,119
설계든, 하드웨어 설계든,
의사나 변호사든, 그렇죠?

779
00:44:49,119 --> 00:44:52,656
그 사람들이 전문가가 되는
건 특화된 도구를 다루는
Those people become experts because they spend time working with specialized tools, they spend time...

780
00:44:52,656 --> 00:44:56,319
데 오랜 시간을 들이기
때문이거든요, 시간을 들여서…

781
00:44:56,319 --> 00:44:57,692
그들한테는 인터넷 한 개 분량의 데이터가
They don't have an internet worth of data — that is why they're experts.

782
00:44:57,692 --> 00:44:59,440
없어요—오히려 그게 그들을
전문가로 만드는 거예요.

783
00:44:59,440 --> 00:45:03,161
그러니까 어떻게 모델을 그
수준까지 끌어올려서, 그
Right, so how can we get models to get to that level, to work alongside humans at that level?

784
00:45:03,161 --> 00:45:07,280
수준에서 사람과 나란히 일하게
할 수 있을까 하는 거죠.

785
00:45:07,280 --> 00:45:09,426
그리고 그게 바로 경제적 가치의 상당
And that's where a lot of the economic value is going to come from, right?

786
00:45:09,426 --> 00:45:11,680
부분이 나올 지점이라고 봐요, 그렇죠?

787
00:45:11,680 --> 00:45:14,486
그리고 당연히 그걸 어떤 방식으로
하든, 그 주변의 하드웨어 최적화도
And obviously then the hardware optimizations you have to do around — whatever the way to do that is — you've worked on state-space based models.

788
00:45:14,486 --> 00:45:17,440
필요할 텐데요—상태공간 모델(SSM)
기반 아키텍처 연구를 해오셨잖아요.

789
00:45:17,440 --> 00:45:20,960
트랜스포머의 대안이 될 아키텍처들을
오래 고민해오신 걸로 아는데요.
You know, you've spent a lot of time thinking about alternative architectures to transformers.

790
00:45:20,960 --> 00:45:23,409
공동 연구자이신 앨버트 구
님도 트랜스포머 단독으로는
You know, I think your collaborator Albert Gu said that transformers are not going to be the final solution by themselves.

791
00:45:23,409 --> 00:45:25,520
최종 해법이 아닐 거라고
하셨던 것 같은데요.

792
00:45:25,520 --> 00:45:29,920
그 수준까지 가려면 아키텍처
혁신이 필요하다고 보시나요?
Like, do you think we need, you know, architectural innovation to get us to that level?

793
00:45:29,920 --> 00:45:32,319
네, 저 앨버트 정말 좋아해요.
Right, I love Albert.

794
00:45:32,319 --> 00:45:36,800
근데 어쩌면 여기서 저희 생각이
좀 갈리는 것 같아요, 그렇죠?
But maybe this is where we disagree a little bit, right?

795
00:45:36,800 --> 00:45:41,840
그러니까 AGI나 ASI에
도달하는 데는 지금
So I think to get to, you know, AGI or ASI, it's possible that the current architecture we have is sufficient, right?

796
00:45:41,840 --> 00:45:46,880
아키텍처로도 충분할 수
있다고 봐요, 그렇죠?

797
00:45:46,880 --> 00:45:49,440
그런데 어떤 대가를 치르고요?
But at what cost?

798
00:45:49,440 --> 00:45:52,640
더 나은 아키텍처가 있으면
1~2년 더 일찍 도달할
Which is, hey, if you have a better architecture, maybe you get there one or two years sooner — that's probably worth it.

799
00:45:52,640 --> 00:45:55,839
수도 있는데, 그건 아마
그럴 가치가 있을 거예요.

800
00:45:55,839 --> 00:46:00,800
아니면 10배 적은 비용으로
도달할 수도 있고요, 그렇죠?
Or you get there with 10x less cost, right?

801
00:46:00,800 --> 00:46:04,000
그리고 그게 결국 ASI에 도달했을
때의 접근성으로 이어지고요.
Which ultimately flows to then the accessibility of that ASI when you get there.

802
00:46:04,000 --> 00:46:08,355
맞아요, 맞아요. 그리고 그게 확실히
중요한 게, 매년 AI 인프라에
Right, right. And that's certainly crucial, because, you know, we're spending — I don't know — $500 billion on AI infrastructure every year.

803
00:46:08,355 --> 00:46:12,480
— 정확히는 모르지만 — 5,000억
달러 정도를 쓰고 있잖아요.

804
00:46:12,480 --> 00:46:15,359
이 숫자를 인용은 하지 마시고요,
대략 그 정도 규모인 것 같아요.
Don't quote me on that, but I think that's the sort of the right ballpark.

805
00:46:15,359 --> 00:46:16,160
네, 맞아요.
Yeah, right.

806
00:46:16,160 --> 00:46:21,040
그럼 그 10배를 써야 할까요?
그건 좀 비현실적으로 보이는데요.
So do we need to spend 10x that, which seems, you know, somewhat unrealistic?

807
00:46:21,040 --> 00:46:24,802
아니면 더 나은 아키텍처로
지금 쓰는 만큼,
Or, with a better architecture, can we get there with the current amount of spending, or maybe even less?

808
00:46:24,802 --> 00:46:28,720
혹은 그보다 적게 쓰고도
도달할 수 있을까요?

809
00:46:28,720 --> 00:46:33,831
그래서 바로 거기서 아키텍처가
역할을 한다고 봐요 —
So I think that's where the architecture is going to play a role, which is — can we get to AGI with a better architecture?

810
00:46:33,831 --> 00:46:39,119
더 나은 아키텍처로 AGI에
도달할 수 있느냐는 거죠.

811
00:46:39,119 --> 00:46:42,400
지금 아키텍처가 필요한 요소는 어느
정도 다 갖추고 있다고 생각해요.
I think the current architecture sort of has all the right ingredients.

812
00:46:42,400 --> 00:46:46,612
계속 스케일업하면 — 다들
그렇게 해왔듯이 — 거기 도달할
And if you keep scaling, which people have been doing, you could get there, but maybe the cost is just a little... astronomical.

813
00:46:46,612 --> 00:46:50,560
수는 있겠지만, 비용이 좀...
천문학적일 수 있어요.

814
00:46:50,560 --> 00:46:52,640
다른 어떤 아키텍처를 주목하고 계신가요?
What other architectures are you paying attention to?

815
00:46:52,640 --> 00:46:56,400
네, 저는 전문가
혼합(MoE)에 꽤 기대하고
Right, so I'm pretty excited about mixture of experts, especially getting sparser and sparser.

816
00:46:56,400 --> 00:47:00,160
있어요, 특히 점점 더
희소해지는 방향으로요.

817
00:47:00,160 --> 00:47:04,075
그 한계를 밀어붙이려고 하고
있어요 — 얼마나 희소하게
We're trying to push that limit — like, how sparse can you go — and we're working on that.

818
00:47:04,075 --> 00:47:07,599
갈 수 있는지 — 저희도
그 작업을 하고 있고요.

819
00:47:07,599 --> 00:47:11,440
꽤 설득력 있는 방향이었다고 생각해요.
I think it's been a compelling direction.

820
00:47:11,440 --> 00:47:14,539
딥시크가 이걸 정말 극도로 희소하게 만들
Some really important work done by DeepSeek showing that you can make these things really, really sparse.

821
00:47:14,539 --> 00:47:17,920
수 있다는 걸 보여준
중요한 연구를 했고요.

822
00:47:17,920 --> 00:47:22,160
그보다 더 일찍 딥마인드도 그
방향으로 밀어붙인 연구가 있었어요.
There was even earlier work from DeepMind pushing in that direction as well.

823
00:47:22,160 --> 00:47:25,412
그래서 이게 같은
연산량으로 더 많은 지능을
So I think that's one compelling way to sort of get more intelligence out for the same compute.

824
00:47:25,412 --> 00:47:28,800
뽑아내는 설득력 있는
방법 중 하나라고 봐요.

825
00:47:28,800 --> 00:47:32,640
결국 저희가 최적화하고 싶은
건 달러당 추론량이에요.
Ultimately, I think we want to optimize inference per dollar.

826
00:47:32,640 --> 00:47:33,119
네, 맞아요.
Yeah, right.

827
00:47:33,119 --> 00:47:37,192
그건 flop당(부동소수점
연산당) 추론량과 달러당
And that means it can be factored into inference per flop — floating point operation — and flops per dollar.

828
00:47:37,192 --> 00:47:41,119
flop, 이렇게 둘로
나눌 수 있다는 뜻이고요.

829
00:47:41,119 --> 00:47:44,479
즉 flop당 추론량은 아키텍처, 설계,
That is, inference per flop is more about architecture, design, data, and algorithms.

830
00:47:44,479 --> 00:47:47,839
데이터, 알고리즘에 더 가까운 문제고요.

831
00:47:47,839 --> 00:47:49,811
반대쪽인 '달러당 flops'는
And then the other side, flops per dollar, is more about hardware and kernel optimization.

832
00:47:49,811 --> 00:47:52,480
하드웨어랑 커널 최적화
쪽에 더 가깝고요.

833
00:47:52,480 --> 00:48:00,640
아키텍처 쪽에서는 같은 연산으로
최대한 많은 지능을 뽑아내려는 거예요.
So on the architecture side, we're trying to extract as much intelligence out of the same computation.

834
00:48:00,640 --> 00:48:02,960
MoE가 그중 하나인 것 같고요.
So mixture of experts is one, I think.

835
00:48:02,960 --> 00:48:07,680
앨버트 구랑 같이한 상태공간 관련
작업들이 정말 정말 재밌었어요.
Some of the state space stuff done with Albert has been really, really fun.

836
00:48:07,680 --> 00:48:10,240
사람들이 그걸 받아들이고
있잖아요, 그렇죠?
And people are picking that up, right?

837
00:48:10,240 --> 00:48:15,280
엔비디아 쪽 분들이랑 실제로 모델
학습하면서 협업도 많이 했고요.
We've collaborated with a bunch of folks from NVIDIA actually training models.

838
00:48:15,280 --> 00:48:18,873
그렇게 정말 정말 고품질 모델을 만들 수
They released several of these where they show they can get really, really high quality models.

839
00:48:18,873 --> 00:48:23,119
있다는 걸 보여주는 결과물을
몇 개 공개했어요.

840
00:48:23,119 --> 00:48:29,040
트랜스포머랑 맘바를 섞은 하이브리드
같은 이런 아키텍처를 써서요.
Using this kind of architecture — it's a hybrid of transformer and Mamba and so on.

841
00:48:29,040 --> 00:48:35,280
근데 비용은 훨씬 적게 들면서
추론 성능은 훨씬훨씬 더 높죠.
But with much smaller cost, or much, much higher inference performance, right?

842
00:48:35,280 --> 00:48:39,760
그래서 추론에 있어서는 아키텍처가
정말 중요한 것 같아요.
So I think architecture is very important for this, for inference.

843
00:48:39,760 --> 00:48:45,040
그게 아키텍처 연구의 효용인 것 같고요.
I think that's the utility of architecture research.

844
00:48:45,040 --> 00:48:47,093
요즘 저는 이걸 정말 많이 생각하는데,
And nowadays I think about it very much — I call it inference-first architecture design.

845
00:48:47,093 --> 00:48:49,440
'추론 우선 아키텍처
설계'라고 부르거든요.

846
00:48:49,440 --> 00:48:52,880
어차피 대부분의 flops가
추론에 쓰이니까요.
Which is, most of the flops are being spent on inference anyway.

847
00:48:52,880 --> 00:48:56,640
그래서 추론이 정말 정말 잘 되도록
아키텍처를 설계하고 싶은 거예요.
So you really, really want to design architecture that makes inference really, really good.

848
00:48:56,640 --> 00:49:00,559
음, 확실히 — 플래시어텐션으로
추론 쪽을 크게 발전시키셨잖아요.
Well, I'm sure — I mean, obviously you've pushed forward the inference side with Flash Attention.

849
00:49:00,559 --> 00:49:02,960
그리고 맘바로 상태공간 모델
쪽에서도 선구자셨고요.
And with Mamba, you've been a pioneer on state space models.

850
00:49:02,960 --> 00:49:05,352
지금 어떤 연구 분야에
관심 있으신지, 시간을
I'm sure folks are curious what research areas are interesting to you now, where you're spending your time.

851
00:49:05,352 --> 00:49:07,839
어디에 쓰고 계신지 다들
궁금해하실 것 같아요.

852
00:49:07,839 --> 00:49:10,559
다음 큰 논문은 어떤
걸 기대하면 될까요?
What's the next big paper we should expect from you?

853
00:49:10,559 --> 00:49:15,680
네, 이 분야들은 여전히
계속 작업하고 있어요.
Right, yeah, I'm still certainly working in these areas.

854
00:49:15,680 --> 00:49:17,920
이 분야들에 정말 관심이 많거든요.
I'm so very much interested in these areas.

855
00:49:17,920 --> 00:49:23,440
근데 새로운 방향들도
좀 탐색하고 있어요.
But I'm exploring some new directions.

856
00:49:23,440 --> 00:49:27,086
그중 일부는 다음으로 정말 임팩트 있을
Some of which involve working with — sort of — what are the next set of really impactful applications.

857
00:49:27,086 --> 00:49:30,559
응용 분야가 뭘지 다루는 작업이고요.

858
00:49:30,559 --> 00:49:32,800
로보틱스가 그중 하나인
것 같아요, 그렇죠?
I think robotics is one of them, right?

859
00:49:32,800 --> 00:49:36,960
로보틱스 얘기하자면 —
가정에서 실제로 쓸 만한
And robotics — like, how far are we away from having really good humanoid robots that work in homes?

860
00:49:36,960 --> 00:49:41,280
정말 좋은 휴머노이드
로봇까지 얼마나 남았을까요?

861
00:49:41,280 --> 00:49:43,280
한 5년, 아니면 10년
정도일 것 같아요.
Maybe five years, maybe 10 years.

862
00:49:43,280 --> 00:49:44,240
네, 저도 잘 모르겠어요, 그쵸?
Yeah, I don't know, right?

863
00:49:44,240 --> 00:49:47,606
그리고 그건 보통 연구
쪽에서 답하고 싶은
And that usually means a lot of interesting and exciting questions on the research side that we want to answer.

864
00:49:47,606 --> 00:49:51,119
흥미롭고 재밌는 질문들이
많다는 뜻이거든요.

865
00:49:51,119 --> 00:49:55,280
그래서 그게 연구 쪽 방향 중 하나예요.
So that's a direction on the research side.

866
00:49:55,280 --> 00:49:57,920
로보틱스 연구 영역 중에서
제일 흥미로운 건 뭔가요?
What's most interesting to you within the robotics research area?

867
00:49:57,920 --> 00:50:01,804
로봇은, 로봇을 제어하는
모델을 이미 있는
So robots — we can initialize a model controlling a robot with some of the foundation models that we already have.

868
00:50:01,804 --> 00:50:05,520
파운데이션 모델들로 초기화할 수 있어요.

869
00:50:05,520 --> 00:50:07,680
언어 모델을 써서 플래닝을 할 수 있죠.
You can use a language model to do the planning.

870
00:50:07,680 --> 00:50:10,477
예를 들어 로봇한테
커피잔을 집으라고 하면,
Like, if you ask a robot to pick up a coffee cup, then the language model can say...

871
00:50:10,477 --> 00:50:13,040
언어 모델이 이렇게 말할 수 있어요...

872
00:50:13,040 --> 00:50:17,200
'저 테이블로 가서 커피를
집어라' 이런 식으로요.
'Go walk over to that table and pick up the coffee,' and so on.

873
00:50:17,200 --> 00:50:21,278
근데 빠진 건 세상과 상호작용하고 실제로
But what's missing, I think, is this kind of interacting and acting in the world, right?

874
00:50:21,278 --> 00:50:24,800
행동하는 부분인 것 같아요, 그쵸?

875
00:50:24,800 --> 00:50:27,760
그건 데이터가 아예 없거든요.
Because we just don't have data for that.

876
00:50:27,760 --> 00:50:31,040
언어 데이터는 있어요 —
언어 쪽 데이터는 있거든요.
We have data on language — we have language data.

877
00:50:31,040 --> 00:50:31,200
그쵸?
Right?

878
00:50:31,200 --> 00:50:33,014
보시다시피 시뮬레이션 데이터를
스케일업하려는 사람들도
You've obviously seen some folks trying to scale simulation data, other folks doing teleop.

879
00:50:33,014 --> 00:50:34,640
있고, 텔레옵(원격조작)을
하는 사람들도 있죠.

880
00:50:34,640 --> 00:50:37,359
근데 실제 구동(액추에이션)
쪽엔 데이터 문제가 있어요,
But there's a data problem on the actual actuation side,

881
00:50:37,359 --> 00:50:37,520
그쵸?
right?

882
00:50:37,520 --> 00:50:38,880
그게 확실히 하나고요.
So that's certainly one.

883
00:50:38,880 --> 00:50:43,920
다른 하나는, 제 생각엔
로봇이 정보를 일종의 다중
The other one is, I think, robots kind of have to process information in a sort of multi-resolution, multi-time-scale way.

884
00:50:43,920 --> 00:50:48,960
해상도, 다중 시간 척도로
처리해야 한다는 거예요.

885
00:50:48,960 --> 00:50:55,280
그러니까 관절을 제어할 땐 아주,
아주 빠르게 반응해야 하고요.
So, if you're controlling the joints, you have to act very, very rapidly.

886
00:50:55,280 --> 00:50:55,599
네.
Yeah.

887
00:50:55,599 --> 00:51:00,079
근데 로봇의 경로를 계획할 땐
훨씬 느리게 해도 되거든요.
But if you're planning a route for the robot, you can do it much slower.

888
00:51:00,079 --> 00:51:01,760
그래서 서로 다른 아키텍처를
쓰고 싶어지는 거죠.
So it's like different architectures you might want to use.

889
00:51:01,760 --> 00:51:03,272
그러고 보니 그게 다오 님이 하신
I mean, I'm struck that seems very analogous to some of the state space model work you've done.

890
00:51:03,272 --> 00:51:05,119
SSM 작업이랑 되게 비슷한 것 같네요.

891
00:51:05,119 --> 00:51:05,599
맞아요, 맞아요.
Right, right.

892
00:51:05,599 --> 00:51:10,353
그러니까 시간 척도를
명시적으로 고려해서,
So, explicitly taking time scale into account, do I want to do very lightweight computation to just control the joints?

893
00:51:10,353 --> 00:51:15,520
관절 제어만 할 땐 아주
가벼운 연산을 할지,

894
00:51:15,520 --> 00:51:19,280
아니면 로봇 경로를 최적화하려고 훨씬
Or do I want to do much heavier-weight reasoning to plan the optimal route for the robot?

895
00:51:19,280 --> 00:51:23,040
무거운 리즈닝을 할지를 정하는 거죠.

896
00:51:23,040 --> 00:51:27,172
그래서 언어 모델, 비전
모델, 오디오 모델, 월드
So I think it's going to be this sort of composite system initialized from language models, vision model, audio model, world model.

897
00:51:27,172 --> 00:51:31,599
모델에서 초기화된 일종의 복합
시스템이 될 것 같아요.

898
00:51:31,599 --> 00:51:33,119
근데 이걸 어떻게 이어붙일까요?
But how do you stitch them together?

899
00:51:33,119 --> 00:51:35,280
그게 큰 질문인 것 같아요.
But I think that's the big question.

900
00:51:35,280 --> 00:51:39,280
로컬에서 충분히 빠르게 돌리려면
흥미로운 하드웨어 질문들도 있고요.
Some fascinating hardware questions to be able to run the stuff locally and fast enough.

901
00:51:39,280 --> 00:51:40,160
네, 네, 네.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

902
00:51:40,160 --> 00:51:42,800
어디서든 원하는 곳에서
일하실 수 있을 것 같은데,
I feel like you could probably work anywhere you want,

903
00:51:42,800 --> 00:51:46,319
정말 흥미로운 건, 물론
투게더 수석 과학자시면서도,
and I think one really interesting thing is you're, obviously, chief scientist at Together,

904
00:51:46,319 --> 00:51:48,559
학계 쪽 길도 함께 걸어오셨다는 거예요,
but you've also, you know, gone the academic route as well,

905
00:51:48,559 --> 00:51:50,720
프린스턴대 조교수시기도 하고요.
and you're assistant professor over at Princeton.

906
00:51:50,720 --> 00:51:53,119
학계에 남기로 한 건
어떻게 생각하신 건가요?
How did you think about staying within academia?

907
00:51:53,119 --> 00:51:56,400
그리고 로보틱스 분야에서
다룰 만한 문제들도요 —
And then also, the set of problems that it makes sense in this robotics space —

908
00:51:56,400 --> 00:51:59,219
피지컬 인텔리전스에서
일하거나, 로보틱스 회사들과
you could obviously go work at Physical Intelligence, or partner with some of these robotics companies, or do the work from an academic setting.

909
00:51:59,219 --> 00:52:02,559
협업하거나, 아니면 학계에서
그 작업을 할 수도 있잖아요.

910
00:52:02,559 --> 00:52:07,200
학계와 업계 중 어디서 하는
게 맞는지는 어떻게 판단하세요?
How do you think about what makes sense to work on within academia versus industry?

911
00:52:07,200 --> 00:52:07,760
네, 네.
Right, right.

912
00:52:07,760 --> 00:52:11,680
네, 좋은 질문이네요. 제 답은,
이건 개인적인 거라는 거예요.
Yeah, this is a great question, and my answer is, it's going to be personal.

913
00:52:11,680 --> 00:52:15,040
사람마다 다를 것 같아요.
For every person, it's going to be different.

914
00:52:15,040 --> 00:52:21,359
저 개인적으로는 스타트업과
교수직 둘 다 하는 걸 좋아해요.
So, for me personally, I actually like being both in a startup and being a professor.

915
00:52:21,359 --> 00:52:25,920
생각하고 실행하는 방식이
서로 다르게 주어지거든요.
That sort of presents different modes of thinking and execution.

916
00:52:25,920 --> 00:52:29,200
스타트업 쪽은 빠르게
움직이니까 정말 재미있어요.
On the startup side, it's very fun because we move fast.

917
00:52:29,200 --> 00:52:29,839
네, 맞아요.
Yeah, right.

918
00:52:29,839 --> 00:52:32,680
하고 싶은 일이 있으면 며칠, 몇 주,
If things we want to do, we could get it done in days or weeks or at most months.

919
00:52:32,680 --> 00:52:35,520
길어야 몇 달 안에 끝낼 수 있거든요.

920
00:52:35,520 --> 00:52:40,640
그 정도 시간 범위로
계획을 세우는 거죠.
That's the level of horizon that we plan for, right?

921
00:52:40,640 --> 00:52:41,839
그리고 정말 재미있어요.
And it's very fun.

922
00:52:41,839 --> 00:52:44,481
정말 뛰어난 팀을 꾸리면 아주 빠르게
You get a team of really good people, they can execute very fast, and you accomplish great things.

923
00:52:44,481 --> 00:52:47,520
실행해서 대단한 일들을
해낼 수 있거든요.

924
00:52:47,520 --> 00:52:52,720
투게더에서 팀이 해온
작업이 정말 자랑스러워요.
And I'm very proud of the work that the team has done at Together.

925
00:52:52,720 --> 00:52:54,880
그래서 정말 재미있었어요.
So that's been very fun.

926
00:52:54,880 --> 00:52:58,994
학계 쪽은 시간 지평이 좀 더 길고,
On the academia side, it's a little bit longer time horizon, and the kind of problems we think about are maybe more speculative.

927
00:52:58,994 --> 00:53:03,520
생각하는 문제들도 더 사변적인 편이에요.

928
00:53:03,520 --> 00:53:07,359
한 달 안에 답이 필요한
문제를 푸는 게 아니거든요.
We don't solve the problem that would need a solution in a month.

929
00:53:07,359 --> 00:53:12,061
세상이 2~3년 뒤에 그
방향으로 간다면, 물어볼
We think about — if the world is moving in that direction in two to three years, what are the interesting questions to ask?

930
00:53:12,061 --> 00:53:16,240
만한 흥미로운 질문이
뭘까 생각하는 거예요.

931
00:53:16,240 --> 00:53:21,200
그리고 1~2년 안에 풀어야 할
과제는 뭘까, 그런 걸 생각하고요.
And what are the challenges to solve in one or two years?

932
00:53:21,200 --> 00:53:24,000
그런 시간 척도로 생각하는 거죠.
And we think in that time scale.

933
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:29,520
그래서 학생들과 함께 일하는 것도
정말 재미있고 보람 있었어요,
So working with students has been very fun and rewarding as well,

934
00:53:29,520 --> 00:53:36,559
본질적으로 흥미로운 질문들을
생각해볼 수 있으니까요.
because we get to think about these questions that are interesting, just intrinsically interesting.

935
00:53:36,559 --> 00:53:43,520
물론 트레이드오프도 있어요 —
학계의 컴퓨트 양은 훨씬 적거든요.
Obviously, there are certain trade-offs — the amount of compute in academia is much smaller.

936
00:53:43,520 --> 00:53:47,994
평가하는 방식도, 정말
빠르게 작동하느냐가
The way you evaluate it is, are your ideas interesting, rather than does it work and run really fast?

937
00:53:47,994 --> 00:53:52,079
아니라 아이디어가 흥미로운가로 하고요.

938
00:53:52,079 --> 00:53:54,480
완전히 다른 평가 기준이죠?
That's a different set of evaluation, right?

939
00:53:54,480 --> 00:53:58,766
그래서 학계에서는 이런
긴 시간 지평의 문제를
So I think in academia you have some more freedom to think deeply about these longer horizon problems.

940
00:53:58,766 --> 00:54:02,880
깊이 생각할 자유가 좀
더 있는 것 같아요.

941
00:54:02,880 --> 00:54:08,160
그리고 저는 두 방식
다 좋아하는 편이에요.
And I happen to like both modes of operation.

942
00:54:08,160 --> 00:54:13,839
그래서 지금도 프린스턴에서
교수로 있는 거고요.
So that's why I'm still at Princeton as a professor.

943
00:54:13,839 --> 00:54:16,640
스타트업에도 계속 관여하고 있어요.
I'm still involved in the startup.

944
00:54:16,640 --> 00:54:16,880
네.
Yeah.

945
00:54:16,880 --> 00:54:17,040
맞아요.
Right.

946
00:54:17,040 --> 00:54:21,845
결국 탐색과 활용을 섞은
것 같아요, 거시적으로 보면
And I think ultimately it's maybe a mix of exploration and exploitation, like if you think at a macro level, academia's role is more on exploration, right?

947
00:54:21,845 --> 00:54:26,319
학계의 역할은 탐색 쪽에
더 가깝잖아요, 그렇죠?

948
00:54:26,319 --> 00:54:29,920
자금은 보통 정부에서 나오고요.
The funding comes from, usually, the government.

949
00:54:29,920 --> 00:54:33,651
정부가 관심 있는 건,
수많은 아이디어를 탐색해서
The government cares about: can you explore tons and tons of ideas to see if maybe five or 10% of those ideas will work out?

950
00:54:33,651 --> 00:54:37,520
그중 5~10%가 성공하는지
볼 수 있느냐는 거죠.

951
00:54:37,520 --> 00:54:43,599
투자자로서, VC로서도 사실 그런
방식으로 움직이시는 거잖아요.
I mean, as an investor, as a VC, that's kind of the mode you operate in as well.

952
00:54:43,599 --> 00:54:47,542
즉, 수많은 아이디어를
탐색하다 보면 그중
Which is: hey, we're going to explore lots of ideas, maybe five or 10 percent of these ideas will become incredibly important, right?

953
00:54:47,542 --> 00:54:52,000
5~10%가 엄청나게
중요해질 수 있다는 거죠?

954
00:54:52,000 --> 00:54:54,160
예를 들어 어텐션이 그런 사례죠?
You know, one example is attention, right?

955
00:54:54,160 --> 00:54:59,250
어텐션은 구글 논문으로 유명해졌지만,
사실 밀라(MILA)에서 즈미트리
Attention, even though it got famous with the Google paper, actually came out of the work at MILA, with Dmitry Bahdanau, Yoshua Bengio, and other folks, right?

956
00:54:59,250 --> 00:55:04,079
바흐다나우, 요슈아 벤지오 같은
분들이 한 작업에서 나온 거잖아요?

957
00:55:04,079 --> 00:55:08,400
학계에서 나온 셈이죠?
It was kind of coming out of academia, right?

958
00:55:08,400 --> 00:55:14,434
지금 아키텍처의 다른 구성
요소들을 봐도, Adam
And if you think of all the other components in the current architecture — the Adam optimizer was Jimmy Ba at Toronto and other folks, right?

959
00:55:14,434 --> 00:55:21,760
옵티마이저도 토론토대의 지미 바랑
다른 분들이 만든 거잖아요?

960
00:55:21,760 --> 00:55:26,079
레이어놈(LayerNorm)도
그렇고요, 그것도 지미 바가 만든 거죠?
LayerNorm too — that's also Jimmy Ba, right?

961
00:55:26,079 --> 00:55:31,485
사실 지금 우리가 쓰는 것들
대부분이 학계에서 나온 거예요,
Essentially, lots of what we have now came out of academia, because the role of academia is to explore lots and lots of ideas.

962
00:55:31,485 --> 00:55:36,400
학계의 역할이 정말 많은
아이디어를 탐색하는 거니까요.

963
00:55:36,400 --> 00:55:38,319
